15:16:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s definitely **not** just a British thing — far from it. The UK is far from unique here; in fact, timber joists and floorboards for upper floors are the standard in many countries, particularly those with a strong wood‑building tradition. The reason you’ve heard it described as “British” is likely because the UK is unusual in one specific combination: heavy, solid **masonry party walls** plus **timber intermediate floors**. In many other countries, if timber is used for floors, the entire house frame is also timber, or the practice is so common it’s never singled out. Let’s clear up the misconception. ### Countries where timber upper floors are common or dominant Here’s a quick overview: | Country/Region | Use of timber upper floors in houses | |---------------|--------------------------------------| | **USA & Canada** | **Overwhelmingly standard.** Light‑frame wood construction means walls, floors and roof are all timber. The vast majority of suburban houses have engineered wood I‑joists or sawn lumber joists with OSB or plywood sheathing. | | **Sweden, Norway, Finland** | **Standard in almost all low‑rise housing.** Even when the building is clad in brick or render, the structural frame is almost always timber, with timber joists and chipboard/plywood floors. | | **Australia & New Zealand** | **Standard.** Brick‑veneer or timber‑frame houses both use timber joist floors, often with particleboard sheets. Concrete floors appear mainly on the ground floor (slab‑on‑ground). | | **Ireland** | Very similar to the UK: masonry walls with suspended timber joists and floorboards for upper storeys. | | **Japan** | Traditional wooden houses use timber joists and boards. Modern post‑and‑beam houses commonly use engineered timber floors. | | **Belgium & Netherlands** | Many houses have brick/render exteriors but timber joist floors. Engineered wood I‑joists are increasingly common. | | **France** | Regional variation, but timber floors (often large‑section old beams) are common in historic houses. In modern timber‑frame or “MOB” (maison ossature bois) houses, timber is the rule. | | **Germany & Austria** | In Ständerbauweise (timber frame) or Fertighaus (prefabricated timber houses), timber joists are standard. In massiv (solid) construction, concrete dominates. So you’ll see both, but timber is far from unusual. | | **Switzerland** | Similar to Germany: many timber‑frame houses with timber floors. Modern sustainable building (“Minergie‑P‑Eco”) strongly promotes timber. | ### The key insight: It’s less about “timber joists” and more about the **wall material** The UK tendency that might seem odd to continental European visitors is this: **Solid brick/block party walls + timber joist floors = a hybrid** In much of mainland Europe (e.g., Germany, Netherlands, Poland), if you build a house with solid masonry or concrete external walls, the ground‑bearing ground floor will be a concrete slab, and upper floors will often be precast concrete planks or in‑situ concrete — because the heavy masonry can carry it, and concrete provides better sound insulation, fire resistance, and thermal mass. But the UK (and Ireland, and some older parts of Belgium/Netherlands) has a long history of brick‑and‑joist terrace housing, originally because timber was cheap, local, and allowed lightweight floors on narrow party walls without needing heavy steel or deep concrete beams. This hybrid persisted well into modern volume housebuilding because of cost and tradition, even though it’s physically unusual when visitors see a heavy blockwork wall supporting nothing but slim timber joists. ### Why you might think “it’s just a British thing” The myth probably persists because: 1. **Scandinavia and North America are seen as “all‑timber” countries** — so the floors blend in and aren’t remarked upon. 2. **In continental Europe, many solid‑masonry houses do use concrete floors**, making the UK’s brick‑and‑joist approach stand out when compared directly to, say, a German *Massivhaus*. 3. **British DIY and housing media** often highlight the “lifting floorboards” trope, reinforcing the idea that it’s a quaint local custom. In reality, anyone in a US home with a plywood subfloor is doing exactly the same thing — they just might unscrew a sheet rather than lift a board. So no, timber joist upper floors are not a British idiosyncrasy. **They’re probably the most common method of suspended residential floor construction in the world.** The UK is just one member of a very large club, and its particular mix of brick walls and timber floors is just a regional flavour of a global standard.
